.TH MYSTRREV 3 "November 2026" "libmyutils 0.4.1" "libmyutils Manual"
.SH NAME
mystrrev \- reverse a string in place
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <mystrfunctions.h>
.sp
.BI "char *mystrrev(char *" str );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B mystrrev
function reverses the null-terminated string pointed to by
.I str
in place, using a two-pointer swap from both ends toward the middle.
The original buffer is modified directly; no new memory is allocated.
.PP
If
.I str
is
.B NULL
the function returns
.B NULL
without modifying anything.
If the string has length 0 or 1, it is returned unchanged.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns a pointer to the same buffer passed in
.RI ( str ),
now containing the reversed string.
Returns
.B NULL
if
.I str
was
.BR NULL .
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include <mystrfunctions.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void) {
    char buf[] = "hello";
    printf("%s\en", mystrrev(buf));  /* prints "olleh" */
    return 0;
}
.fi
.SH NOTES
Because the reversal happens in place, callers must pass a mutable,
writable buffer (not a string literal).
.SH AUTHOR
Written as part of the OS Assignment 01: Core C Utilities Library.
